{
19: {skill_tested: "Identify a detail that explains the narrator's reaction.", question: "According to Passage A, why does the narrator initially feel more like an exhibit label than a useful visitor?", option1: "Mara records the narrator's explanation instead of using the old tool immediately.", option2: "The apprentices refuse to follow the narrator's instructions about the doorway.", option3: "The renovated theater no longer contains a backstage work area.", option4: "Mara asks the narrator to leave before the theater reopens.", correctAnswer: "Mara records the narrator's explanation instead of using the old tool immediately.", explanation: "Step 1: The narrator expects identifying the wooden wheel to produce a sense of usefulness.<br><br>Step 2: Mara photographs the wheel and stores the explanation in an archive, making the narrator feel recorded as part of the past rather than involved in present work.<br><br>Answer: Mara records the narrator's explanation instead of using the old tool immediately.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
20: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of a figurative word.", question: "As used in the final paragraph of Passage B, the word 'roots' most nearly refers to the narrator's:", option1: "continuing dependence on Mrs. Okafor's approval.", option2: "earlier experience that helped shape current expertise.", option3: "plan to replace the greenhouse's existing plants.", option4: "reluctance to accept professional responsibility.", correctAnswer: "earlier experience that helped shape current expertise.", explanation: "Step 1: The narrator carries both the seedling tray associated with earlier volunteer work and the plans associated with current professional work.<br><br>Step 2: The narrator recognizes that the earlier experience contributed to the present design, so 'roots' refers to that formative experience.<br><br>Answer: earlier experience that helped shape current expertise.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
21: {skill_tested: "Identify a central idea shared by paired passages.", question: "Which statement best expresses a central idea shared by both passages?", option1: "Institutions preserve their traditions most effectively by resisting technological change.", option2: "Professional expertise is weakened when it becomes connected to personal memory.", option3: "Returning to former work can reveal that past experience remains useful in altered circumstances.", option4: "People understand their former institutions better when they avoid participating in current work.", correctAnswer: "Returning to former work can reveal that past experience remains useful in altered circumstances.", explanation: "Step 1: In Passage A, the narrator's inherited method helps the apprentices repair a frame.<br><br>Step 2: In Passage B, the narrator's volunteer knowledge strengthens a professional design.<br><br>Answer: Returning to former work can reveal that past experience remains useful in altered circumstances.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
22: {skill_tested: "Infer how a character's actions revise the narrator's interpretation.", question: "In Passage A, the apprentices' arrival primarily changes the narrator's understanding of the visit by showing that:", option1: "Mara lacks the technical knowledge required to supervise them.", option2: "the theater's digital archive contains inaccurate descriptions.", option3: "the apprentices prefer traditional tools to modern equipment.", option4: "practical knowledge can continue through use even when older customs disappear.", correctAnswer: "practical knowledge can continue through use even when older customs disappear.", explanation: "Step 1: Before the apprentices arrive, the narrator experiences the old tools and archived explanation as evidence of being confined to the past.<br><br>Step 2: Repairing the doorway lets the narrator transmit a learned method to new workers.<br><br>Step 3: That transfer supports the later realization that continuity does not require the institution to remain visually unchanged.<br><br>Answer: practical knowledge can continue through use even when older customs disappear.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
23: {skill_tested: "Determine the function of concrete contrasting details.", question: "The references in Passage A to Mara's tablet, the illuminated floor panels, and Mr. Vale's brass keys primarily serve to:", option1: "contrast institutional methods while preparing the question of what can persist through change.", option2: "establish the narrator's belief that the renovated theater is unsafe.", option3: "demonstrate that Mara intends to remove every object associated with Mr. Vale.", option4: "explain why the theater hired apprentices rather than experienced workers.", correctAnswer: "contrast institutional methods while preparing the question of what can persist through change.", explanation: "Step 1: The tablet and floor lights represent current systems, while the brass keys and counted paces represent earlier practices.<br><br>Step 2: The passage later distinguishes vanished routines from knowledge that can still be transmitted.<br><br>Step 3: The early contrast therefore establishes the change against which the narrator eventually identifies a less visible continuity.<br><br>Answer: contrast institutional methods while preparing the question of what can persist through change.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
24: {skill_tested: "Trace the relationship between an initial concern and later evidence.", question: "How does the planning meeting in Passage B affect the narrator's earlier desire to appear exclusively professional?", option1: "It confirms that the board distrusts knowledge gained through volunteer work.", option2: "It shows that practical experience and technical calculations can jointly support professional authority.", option3: "It causes the narrator to abandon the rainwater design in favor of Mrs. Okafor's proposal.", option4: "It persuades the narrator to conceal all previous connections to the greenhouse.", correctAnswer: "It shows that practical experience and technical calculations can jointly support professional authority.", explanation: "Step 1: The narrator initially fears that Mrs. Okafor's story will make the design seem sentimental rather than professional.<br><br>Step 2: During questions, knowledge of both plant needs and calculations allows the narrator to explain the design more convincingly.<br><br>Step 3: The meeting therefore revises the assumed conflict between personal experience and professional expertise.<br><br>Answer: It shows that practical experience and technical calculations can jointly support professional authority.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
25: {skill_tested: "Compare the narrators' initial responses to links with their former work.", question: "At first, both narrators respond to evidence connecting their past and present roles with:", option1: "pride that their former supervisors have preserved every working method.", option2: "certainty that the institutions value memory more than practical results.", option3: "concern that the connection will limit how their present contribution is understood.", option4: "relief that someone else will assume responsibility for their work.", correctAnswer: "concern that the connection will limit how their present contribution is understood.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A's narrator feels reduced to an archival label when treated as a source about an obsolete tool.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B's narrator worries that the board will see the design as an affectionate former volunteer's project rather than professional work.<br><br>Step 3: Both concerns arise because a past identity initially seems capable of narrowing recognition of present usefulness.<br><br>Answer: concern that the connection will limit how their present contribution is understood.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
26: {skill_tested: "Synthesize how parallel objects support different but related realizations.", question: "The straight doorway in Passage A and the seedling tray in Passage B are most similar in that each:", option1: "proves that the institution's newest procedures are less reliable than its former ones.", option2: "causes the narrator to seek formal recognition from the institution's leaders.", option3: "reminds the narrator of a task that was never completed successfully.", option4: "provides tangible evidence that earlier experience can contribute to present work.", correctAnswer: "provides tangible evidence that earlier experience can contribute to present work.", explanation: "Step 1: The doorway remains square because the narrator applies and passes on a method learned at the theater.<br><br>Step 2: The tray embodies the narrator's earlier cultivation work and accompanies the current architectural plans.<br><br>Step 3: Each object therefore turns an abstract connection between past and present into visible evidence of continuing usefulness.<br><br>Answer: provides tangible evidence that earlier experience can contribute to present work.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
27: {skill_tested: "Compare how each passage qualifies the idea of institutional continuity.", question: "Taken together, the endings of the passages most strongly support which conclusion about continuity?", option1: "Continuity can survive visible change when experience is adapted, transmitted, or incorporated into new work.", option2: "Continuity depends on preserving the physical setting in which knowledge was first acquired.", option3: "Continuity is meaningful only when an institution formally records every former worker's contribution.", option4: "Continuity prevents people from developing identities beyond their earliest responsibilities.", correctAnswer: "Continuity can survive visible change when experience is adapted, transmitted, or incorporated into new work.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A rejects the idea that continuity requires old keys, old routes, or an unchanged building; the learned method continues through new apprentices.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B rejects the idea that professional growth requires concealing volunteer experience; that experience becomes part of a new design.<br><br>Step 3: Both endings redefine continuity as transformation and use rather than exact preservation.<br><br>Answer: Continuity can survive visible change when experience is adapted, transmitted, or incorporated into new work.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
}
